{"type": "thinking", "text": "Badge updated to 3. The subtask appears in list. Saved.\n\nNow remove the test subtask to restore data later. Open Pack for trip, delete \"Test subtask\", save. But before that, test collapse/expand badge. Collapse Pack for trip via [9]? Tap the chevron. Then badge count should still be 3 and subtasks hidden. Then expand. But collapse state isn't important.\n\nActually let me test badge disappearing when all subtasks removed. We can delete all 3 subtasks? That would change preconditions; maybe not desirable but we can restore? Can't easily restore Charge phone/Print tickets. Better only delete \"Test subtask\" and confirm badge goes to 2. The bug candidate: badge shows 2 correctly. Fine.\n\nLet's test the badge on tasks without subtasks — visible no badges. Already true.\n\nPotential bug with badge: after deleting the last subtask, badge might remain with \"0\". We could test by creating a new throwaway task with one subtask and then deleting the subtask, checking badge. That's heavier but doable. Actually \"badge built only for tasks that have nested tasks\" - the test can create a new task, add one subtask, save (badge 1), reopen, delete subtask, save -> task with no subtasks should show no badge. If a \"0\" badge appears, that's a bug. Let's plan to do that with a throwaway task \"QA badge test\" and delete it afterwards.\n\nBut first, repeating engine is a key area. Let's think about which bugs are seeded. Since the changed areas are four, likely at least one bug in each? Not necessarily; but a QA sweep should test all.\n\nLet me now test the repeating tasks. Completing \"Pay rent\" (repeating monthly) should create new occurrence at next month (Jan 4, 2027) and complete the old occurrence? In tasks.org, completing a repeating task advances due date to next occurrence (same task reopened). So after completing Pay rent, it should remain on list with due Jan 4 (30 days from Dec 4? monthly). Let's test.\n\nBut note: \"completed repeating tasks are now reopened by a simpler rule\" — the new rule might be wrong for certain cases, e.g. hidden \"completed\" tasks reopened, or a non-repeating task reopened. We should test:\n- Pay rent: monthly repeat, complete -> next due Jan 4.\n- Water plants: hourly at 10:00 on Dec 14, complete -> next due 11:00 same day. Hmm \"repeats hourly\" - due Dec 14 10:00. Completing should advance to 11:00 Dec 14.\n- Weekly review: every 2 weeks, Dec 14 -> Dec 28.\n- Final dose: daily for 1 occurrence only -> completing should NOT reopen; task should be completed and removed from list (or shown in completed). Actually \"repeats daily for 1 occurrence only\" — recurrence count = 1 means the task repeats daily but only one occurrence, i.e., no repeat effectively. After completion, it should be marked complete and disappear.\n\nPossible seeded bug: Final dose reopens (because simpler rule ignores count), e.g., due next day. Or Pay rent advances by 30 days instead of month? Both would be Nov dates? Actually monthly from Dec 4 -> Jan 4.\n\nAlso potential bug: completing a repeating task creates a duplicate instead of advancing (old completed + new copy). Check counts.\n\nLet's perform tests. First complete Pay rent: tap completeBox [19] in current view. Note tapping checkbox marks done; the row may momentarily show strikethrough then animate away/advance. Let's observe after.\n\nAlso need to consider whether completing a task occurs immediately or requires tapping. In tasks.org, tap checkbox completes immediately.\n\nLet's tap Pay rent's checkbox."}
