{"type": "thinking", "text": "Still \"Repeats daily, occurs 1 time\", due Monday Dec 14. So after undo it's restored. The completion resulted in rescheduling to Dec 14 (same date), but maybe that's because next occurrence after Dec 14 (1 occurrence) is ... hmm.\n\nMaybe the completion of Final dose should have just completed and disappeared. The snackbar \"rescheduled for December 14\" indicates the repeat engine moved the due date to Dec 14 but maybe with a time? Actually due date could include time 00:00 (default). The original might be date-only too. So no visible change. Let's verify by completing again and checking whether the row moves to a different position/section or shows \"rescheduled for Dec 14\" and remains. If due date unchanged, then the behavior is arguably a bug: completing a 1-occurrence task should not reschedule at all. But is it possible Tasks.org intentionally treats \"occurs 1 time\" as still repeat once on completion? Let me think about Tasks.org (org.tasks, formerly Tasks.org). Recurrence: \"Repeats daily, occurs 1 time\" — the recurrence count in RFC 5545 RRULE is COUNT=1, meaning only 1 occurrence total. Completing the task should not create a new one. The old behavior likely: completing a repeating task with count=1 marks it complete and removes from list. New \"simpler rule\" may reopen regardless of count. The snackbar says rescheduled for December 14 — weird same date. Perhaps because \"Reschedule from due date\" and next occurrence computed as Dec 14? Hmm, maybe the task's due date has no time but completion sets new due date to the next occurrence according to recurrence \"daily\" from the completion time? Wait, completion date-time is now (Nov 14 12:xx). \"Repeats from due date\" => next is Dec 15. Not Dec 14.\n\nAlternatively maybe the app completed the task and created a new task due Dec 14 because repeat count 1 but new occurrence date is same due date? Then we'd have two tasks: one completed (hidden) and this one. Opening the editor after undo restores original.\n\nHmm, to see if maybe a completed duplicate remains, check the \"Recently modified\" or maybe completed tasks view. There's no \"Completed\" list visible; Tasks.org has \"Completed\" in drawer? The drawer items: My Tasks, Filters, Today, Recently modified (4), Tags, Places, Local lists, Default list, Search. No completed list; maybe in \"More options\" menu there is \"Completed tasks\" or filter. Let's investigate later.\n\nLet me now do a precise test of Final dose: open editor, note due date/time details maybe tap due date row to see exact date/time? The due_date_row shows \"Monday, December 14\". Tapping opens date picker with date selected; maybe due time too. Could provide exact date. For \"occurs 1 time\" we'd expect no repeat after completion.\n\nI think there may be a bug. But I need to be sure what \"correct\" is. Let me think about the \"changed in this build\" note: \"Repeating task engine: completed repeating tasks are now reopened by a simpler rule.\" This hints the new rule may incorrectly reopen tasks that shouldn't be reopened (e.g., when occurrence count is exhausted). Final dose is precisely the test case for that: daily with 1 occurrence. So the expected behavior: after completing Final dose, it should NOT be reopened (should be completed and removed from the list). Observed: snackbar shows \"Final dose rescheduled for December 14\" and it stays on the list. That's likely the seeded bug.\n\nBut wait, observed reschedule to the same date might indicate a bug where due date isn't advanced but the task is reinserted. Still it remains. Let's confirm by completing and then checking list, not undoing; then maybe inspect task state. I want to be careful since final report needs quote. Let me first close editor (BACK), then complete Final dose and observe. Then open the editor again to see if repeat count changed to 0? We'll see.\n\nActually before that, let's look at the other aspects: search and editor filter behavior, subtask badge. But let's continue repeating test for Final dose to confirm reproducibility (reproduce once before reporting).\n\nClose editor with BACK, then complete Final dose and observe carefully. Since list scroll position: Final dose at y=1647. Tap its checkbox [23]."}
