{"type": "thinking", "text": "Search filter persists with \"milk\" and Buy milk result. Good.\n\nNow test \"reapplied whenever the editor is opened\" perhaps by editing a task while filtered and saving. Let's change something? We could edit Buy milk notes then save, but no need. Maybe add a subtask to Buy milk from search results; the task still matches \"milk\". Hmm.\n\nWhat would a filter reapplication bug look like? Suppose search \"milk\" and open Buy milk editor, then via the editor change the due date or title so it no longer matches. Returning should reapply filter. Let's do a safe edit: change title from \"Buy milk\" to \"Buy milk QA\" then save; search filter \"milk\" still matches, so no visible filter bug. To test reapply, we'd want to change title to something not matching \"milk\", then the row should disappear. E.g., change title to \"Buy QA\" and expect search results empty. Then change back. That tests filter reapplication and state. But the editor might not reapply search filter, showing the item still in the list -> bug. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe the described \"active list filter\" refers to the filter chip at the top? Let's check what the search screen with toolbar looks like. Also the top appbar has \"My Tasks\" title and a settings gear. The search bar replaced title. No filter chip.\n\nMaybe the \"active list filter\" means the selected list in the navigation drawer (\"Default list\" or \"My Tasks\"). The editor lists \"Default list\". When you open editor, it reapplies the active filter. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the drawer items: there's \"My Tasks 7\", \"Today\", \"Recently modified 4\", \"Tags\", \"Places\", \"Local lists > Default list 1\". If we navigate to Default list, the list shows only tasks in Default list. Opening editor and saving a task with list = Default list should keep. If we use the editor to change the list to another list, the task should disappear when the filter reapplies. But there's only Default list. Could create a new list. Heavy.\n\nAlternatively \"active list filter\" could be the search query. I think search is the most plausible \"filter\". Note in Tasks.org, search is a \"filter\" internally (Filter class with search term). The editor's filter reapplication would mean when you open the editor from a search result, then go back, the search filter is re-applied. We verified it persists, so fine.\n\nBut a subtle bug: when opening editor from a search result, the editor's \"Save\" might close search? Not in our test (we pressed Back, not Save). Let's test Save path: open Buy milk again from filtered list, then tap Save (no changes), and check filter persists. Tapping Save with no changes might close editor and keep search. Let's test. Currently search active showing Buy milk. Tap row [5]. Then Save [23]."}
