{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! \"Test subtask \" is now shown under \"No due date\" as a *top-level* task! That's a bug: creating/saving a new task while a subtask was pending? Wait, how did Test subtask become a top-level task? In the list, it's under \"No due date\" along with QA badge and Read docs (indented at x=540? The row is at x=540 which is top-level, not 566). It moved out of Pack for trip and is now a top-level task with no due date.\n\nWhat happened? Let's think: When I added the subtask, I typed \"Test subtask\" and pressed ENTER which inserted a newline (\"Test subtask\\n\") and left it focused. Then ESCAPE dismissed keyboard, then Save. It saved as subtask of Pack for trip (shown indented under Pack). Later, when creating \"QA badge\" new task, somehow the subtask was detached? Hmm.\n\nWait, the \"Test subtask\" row now appears under \"No due date\" at x=540 with no indentation? The row body at (540,2052) same as top-level rows. Yes it's top-level. But earlier after saving Pack for trip it was at (566,1325) indented. So it got detached from Pack for trip.\n\nHow? Perhaps my creation of \"QA badge\" was actually *not* a new top-level task but an edit of... no. Hmm.\n\nMaybe this is an artifact of the app's data model: when a subtask's title ends with a newline or is empty? No.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when I created a new task by tapping fab while the \"Pack for trip\" subtask row was in a weird state? No, we were on the main list.\n\nLet's consider sequence right before creating QA badge:\n- Undo Charge phone, list restored with Pack badge 3 and Test subtask at [15].\n- Tap fab [35] -> new task editor (title \"Task name\").\n- Type \"QA badge\".\n- Tap Save [53].\n- Result: QA badge created as top-level (correct), Test subtask detached to top-level (bug).\n\nHmm, why would saving a new task detach a subtask? That seems bizarre. Unless the \"Test subtask\" was never really a subtask of Pack for trip but appeared indented due to being a sibling with parent? Wait, initial creation: In Pack for trip's editor, I tapped \"Add subtask\", typed into the new EditText, pressed ENTER (which inserted newline, didn't create another row), ESCAPE, Save. The list showed \"Test subtask \" at x=566 indented under Pack. So yes it was a subtask.\n\nThen later, I opened and saved Buy milk editor (no changes), searched, etc. Did that detach it? At the observation after exiting search (before subtask completion), Test subtask was still under Pack. Then completed Charge phone and undone. Then created QA badge. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the undo of Charge phone completion caused the detach? Let's see: after undo, the observation showed [15] \"Test subtask \" at (566,1325) under Pack — still a subtask. So no.\n\nThen new task creation... Hmm, could the new task \"QA badge\" have been created while the subtask title field had focus? No, we were in main list.\n\nLet me look at the current state carefully: The \"No due date\" group now contains QA badge, Test subtask, Read docs. Pack for trip shows badge 2 (Charge phone, Print tickets). So Test subtask lost its parent and due date (became no due date). This is a data-corruption bug. But how reproducible? We need to reproduce the detach to file a solid report. Maybe the bug is: saving a new task from the FAB while there's a subtask *draft* pending? Hmm.\n\nWait! Actually maybe the Test subtask title includes a trailing newline, and when the new task editor saved, something parsed the title? No.\n\nLet me reconsider: When I pressed ENTER in the subtask editor, the text became \"Test subtask\\n\" — the newline remains in the title. The list shows \"Test subtask \" (trailing space, likely newline rendered). Could the newline in the title have caused the subtask to be misparsed when the list was rebuilt after creating a new task? That seems unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the detach happened because of *sorting*: Hmm no, \"No due date\" group placement means due date null, not just order.\n\nMaybe I accidentally created \"Test subtask\" as a top-level task from the start? Let's check the first save result: observation after saving Pack for trip:\n[7] Pack for trip badge 3\n[11] Charge phone @566,1021\n[13] Print tickets @566,1173\n[15] Test subtask @566,1325\nSo indented, child.\n\nHmm, what could have changed parentId? Creating and saving another task shouldn't touch existing tasks. Unless the app's \"add subtask\" feature held an in-memory draft that got saved weirdly. But we saved Pack for trip and it persisted across many screens.\n\nCould it be that when I tapped \"Save\" on the new task editor, the app actually saved the *Test subtask* as a new task because the new task editor had somehow loaded the subtask? No, the title was \"QA badge\" and the new row is QA badge. Both exist now.\n\nStrange. Let me look at \"Recently modified\" maybe to see history? Not needed.\n\nWe may have discovered a real bug but non-reproducible. Let's try to reproduce the detach carefully in a controlled way with another throwaway task, but first let's restore this test data:\n- Delete \"Test subtask\" (now top-level) — it was our test artifact. Also delete \"QA badge\". Good, cleaning up.\n- The real pack subtasks remain Charge phone, Print tickets (badge 2) — matches preconditions.\n- Then test again: create subtask \"XYZ\" under Pack, save, then create another top-level task and see if XYZ is detached.\n\nBut before deleting, maybe open \"Test subtask\" and check its details: title and list, maybe due date. Tap [27]. Let's inspect parent? It's now top-level. Maybe it's now a subtask of QA badge? The row at x=540 suggests top-level. QA badge at x=540 too. Hmm.\n\nActually wait: maybe the rows are ordered alphabetically within the \"No due date\" group: QA badge, Test subtask, Read docs — alphabetical? Q, T, R would be Q, R, T, not Q, T, R. So not sorted alphabetically. Maybe by creation date. QA badge created now; Test subtask before; Read docs original. So order by creation date descending? QA badge newest, Test subtask next, Read docs oldest. That matches. So Test subtask is a top-level task in the No due date group.\n\nLet me tap Test subtask to open it and inspect its details (maybe list = Default). Actually, maybe it's not detached: Perhaps it's a *subtask of QA badge*? The row x=566 would indicate child, but it's 540. So top-level.\n\nWe should delete it to restore. But first, maybe this is exactly the seeded \"subtask badge\" bug? \"Subtask badges on task rows: the badge is now built only for tasks that have nested tasks.\" Hmm not related to detach.\n\nHold on, maybe I misread earlier. Let me revisit the earlier observation after saving Pack for trip:\n[15] RelativeLayout contains \"Test subtask \" id=rowBody [clickable] @(566,1325)\nThe x=566 is the same x as Charge phone (566) and Print tickets (566), which are subtasks. So yes child.\n\nNow it's at 540. Detached.\n\nCould tapping \"Save\" in *new task* editor have triggered a \"move completed subtasks\" behavior? Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe this is due to the completion/undo of Charge phone interacting: The undo restored Charge phone, but maybe it restored all subtasks incorrectly including Test subtask with no parent? But at post-undo observation, Test subtask was still under Pack (x=566). Hmm, but maybe the visual grouping is based on sort order and indentation from parentId; if parentId was null yet due date was Pack's due date, it would appear under Pack's due date group but not indented. It was at x=566, so indented.\n\nThen after creating QA badge, it moved groups (due date null). So both parentId and dueDate changed. What operation changes a task's due date and parent? Perhaps the \"Save\" of Pack for trip with Test subtask happened later? No.\n\nCould it be that the app auto-saved the Test subtask as a top-level due to *deleting the newline*? No.\n\nHmm, maybe I accidentally tapped something. When saving QA badge, I tapped element [53] \"Save\". That's the save button. Fine.\n\nAlternatively — wait. Did I create QA badge as a *subtask* of Test subtask? No.\n\nLet me consider that the app might have a \"quick add\" behavior: when you tap FAB, it opens editor; if you had a task selected, e.g., \"Test subtask\" was focused... not.\n\nOK. Let me inspect Test subtask details now, then delete both test tasks. Then attempt reproductio", "truncated": true}
