{"type": "thinking", "text": "Restored preconditions minus the appended newline artifact: Pack badge 2, no extra tasks. Good.\n\nNow let's reproduce the detach hypothesis: create a subtask properly using the IME \"Done\" action rather than ENTER newline. Then save. Then create a new top-level task and save; see if subtask detaches.\n\nBut wait — maybe the detach is caused by something else: my earlier new task \"QA badge\" had no due date, and the subtask \"Test subtask\\n\" has no due date? No, it had Pack's due date (inherited? subtasks in Tasks.org can have independent due date; when adding a subtask, it defaults to no due date? Hmm, but it was grouped under Pack's Due Tuesday header and indented. So it likely inherited due date from parent? Actually in Tasks.org, subtasks don't necessarily inherit due dates; when adding a subtask within parent's editor, maybe the new subtask gets the parent's due date? It showed under Pack's due-date group. Yes.\n\nNow, the new task \"QA badge\" with no due date may have triggered something: maybe the app inserted the new task into the \"No due date\" group and the Test subtask was in the process of being resorted, and it moved? But parent shouldn't change.\n\nCould it be that the \"Test subtask\" with title ending in newline was treated as *two* tasks? When saving Pack, maybe the multline title \"Test subtask\\n\" split into \"Test subtask\" as subtask and empty? Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Maybe the \"Test subtask\" we saw under No due date was not the same entity. When I deleted it via editor, its title was \"Test subtask\\n\". So same entity.\n\nI wonder if the bug is: completing and undoing a subtask causes its sibling (the last subtask?) to be moved to top-level? Let's look at timing: After undo, the list showed Test subtask still indented. But perhaps the visual grouping persisted from before the list refresh? The undo observation was immediate. Then we tapped fab and created QA badge, causing a full refresh; that's when the detach became visible. So the detach may have happened at undo, but only visually materialized after refresh! That's plausible: Undo restored the Charge phone completion and maybe the list model placed Test subtask incorrectly; only after a full reload did it show.\n\nHmm, but the underlying parentId would be read from DB. If undo operation rewrote Test subtask's parent to null, the immediate list might still show it as child due to stale in-memory row? Unclear.\n\nLet's test directly: complete a subtask, undo, then pull-to-refresh or reopen the list (e.g., navigate to drawer and back), and see if the last subtask detaches. That's a reproducible test without creating extra tasks! But we must avoid damaging real data; if it detaches Charge phone or Print tickets, we'd need to restore parent (can we? The editor has no \"parent\" field; deleting/re-adding would be needed. Not good).\n\nBetter to test with a temporary subtask under Pack: add \"QA sub\", save, then complete/undo? Hmm, but we need to test whether create-new-top-level causes detach (maybe that was coincidence). Let's design:\n\nTest A: Add a temporary subtask \"QA sub\" under Pack for trip (properly committed). Save. Verify badge 3 and indented row. Then create a top-level temp task \"QA top\". Save. Observe whether QA sub stays under Pack. If it detaches -> bug reproducible (likely due to \"add subtask\" draft state? Hmm).\n\nActually, I realize there might be a more specific cause: when adding the subtask, I pressed ENTER which inserted a newline instead of committing. Then ESCAPE and Save. What if the app, when saving the parent, saved the subtask *twice*: once as a subtask and once as a top-level task? But we only saw one Test subtask. Hmm.\n\nOr maybe pressing ENTER in the subtask field triggers \"add another subtask\" which created a *second* empty subtask; ESCAPE dismissed; Save then saved both, and the empty one... no.\n\nLet's simply reproduce step-by-step with a fresh subtask and observe at each stage, using proper commit via the IME Done key (key 34). The type_text tool can submit with Enter (submit=true), but that may insert newline too. Better to press the IME \"Done\" button (key 34) after typing. Or tap elsewhere? Let's first add \"QA sub\" to Pack for trip via editor.\n\nCurrent list: Pack at [2]. Tap it."}
