 original order including Test subtask nested.\n5. Created QA badge, saved. Test subtask now top-level.\nSo the detach happened at steps 3-5. The new task save at 5 triggered a reload that revealed detach caused by undo (4)?\n\nHmm. In the current reproduction, we did:\n1. Added QA sub (via parent editor), saved, nested, badge 3.\n2. Created QA top, saved -> QA sub top-level.\n\nWe did NOT complete/undo a subtask this time. So the detach occurs at step 2 (new task creation) or the reload it causes. But QA sub was nested after step 1 and persisted through? We only had one observation after step 1 (immediately after save) and then created QA top. No other reload in between. So the DB state may have been wrong all along; the immediate list display after saving parent might have shown it nested due to in-memory model, not DB. In the earlier Test subtask case, there *were* intervening reloads (search) and it stayed nested, which contradicts \"DB wrong all along\". Unless those searches didn't reload parent-child? But search showed Test subtask in search results? No—search for \"milk\", \"guide\", etc. didn't match Test subtask, so the search list didn't include Pack for trip children except when query maybe matched? The search \"guide\" results only Read docs. Then after exiting search, the main list reloaded and showed Test subtask nested. Hmm, that's a strong indication it was persisted as child.\n\nUnless the list retains in-memory cache across search. The main list's LiveData could keep the same objects and not requery. Possible.\n\nAnyway, the bug is reproducible with the steps: Add a subtask to a task, save, then create a new task from FAB; the subtask appears as top-level under No due date after the new task save. We need to decide whether to report as \"subtask not persisted / added as top-level task\". Let me reproduce one more time cleanly with another fresh subtask, and also try a *reload without creating a new task* to isolate.\n\nCurrent state: Pack badge 2 (Charge phone, Print tickets), QA sub top-level, QA top top-level. Let's clean up later.\n\nTest 2: Add \"QA sub2\" to Pack, save. Then force reload without creating task: open drawer, tap \"Default list\" (or \"My Tasks\")? Navigating to Default list shows tasks in default list; maybe includes all. Then go back to My Tasks. See if QA sub2 is nested or top-level. That will isolate persistence vs new task trigger.\n\nBut wait, Task.org's task list is a LiveData; navigating to another list and back creates a new list instance and reads DB. Good.\n\nLet's do it. First, the QA sub editor is open. Let's close (BACK) and delete both QA tasks later. Actually, we can leave them for now and clean up at the end of the test.\n\nPress BACK. Then Pack for trip row [2] etc. Let me observe first."}
