{"type": "thinking", "text": "Read docs is at the bottom of No due date after QA top2. So sort order maybe by due time or title? Whatever.\n\nNow I want to understand the \"Default list\" discrepancy. Let's open an original task editor (from My Tasks) and tap its list chip to see the list picker. Scroll to top and open Buy milk.\n\nBut more importantly, let's get back to the detach bug. I want to confirm the reproduction and file it. The steps:\n1. Open Pack for trip (has subtasks).\n2. Add a subtask, save.\n3. Verify it appears nested.\n4. Create a new task via FAB, save.\n5. The subtask is not nested; it's at top level under No due date with no due date.\n\nThis is solid. But which area does it belong to? \"subtasks\" area. The symptom: newly added subtask is not persisted as a subtask / gets detached when another task is created. The `symptom_type` could be \"not_persisted\" or \"wrong_navigation\"? \"not_persisted\" fits (subtask parent not persisted). The element_ref for the observation where the defect shows: in the latest observation, the detached QA sub2 row [21] under No due date, and Pack for trip not visible (scrolled). Better use an observation where both Pack badge and detached row are visible. After saving QA top2, the list showed Pack badge 2 (but QA sub2 not visible in same observation because below fold? Actually after saving QA top2, the observation had 35 elements: Pack [7] badge 2, No due date header [29], but rows below not visible. So I don't have a single observation with both. The latest observation shows QA sub2 top-level at [21]. I could file with screen \"My Tasks\" and element_ref 21, observed_quote \"QA sub2\" but the quote alone doesn't show the defect (it's just a task row). The expected/observed fields can explain. Hmm, a report about the detached subtask might be rejected if the quote doesn't show the defect. The instruction: \"point at the element ... and quote the exact on-screen text that shows it\". For a missing-parent issue, the closest element is the row under No due date. The quote \"QA sub2\" under \"No due date\" shows the subtask displayed as top-level. But is that convincing? The observation's position (x=540) and group indicate top-level.\n\nAlternatively, file the bug as \"subtask is not persisted under parent; after adding subtask and saving, creating a new task shows the subtask as a top-level task under No due date\". Element ref 21 (QA sub2 row) with observed_quote \"QA sub2\". Steps detailed. Might be accepted.\n\nBefore filing, let me confirm this isn't an artifact of the specific way I created subtasks. Both times I used Add subtask and pressed Done (which creates a new empty row), then Save. The subtask was nested immediately. It detached upon new task creation. Reproduced twice (Test subtask and QA sub2). Good.\n\nBut wait — maybe the detach is caused by the *empty subtask row* left in the editor when saving? Both times, pressing Done created a new empty subtask row; I pressed BACK to hide keyboard, leaving the empty row, then saved. Maybe the bug: the empty row and the new task creation... Hmm, but why would that matter later? Could be the bug: when saving parent with an empty subtask row, the subtask's parentId isn't stored? Then why nested immediately? Ugh.\n\nTo be rigorous, test a third time but this time remove the empty row before saving (tap the Delete button on the empty row, or tap elsewhere?). Then save, create a new task, check. If it still detaches, solid. If not, the trigger is the empty row + save. Either way it's a bug worth reporting, but the repro steps differ.\n\nLet's examine the *empty row*: After pressing Done, an empty EditText row [54]/[18] was present with a \"Delete\" button. When saving, the empty row is ignored (didn't create an empty task). But maybe the app's subtask insertion logic inserts the *new subtask* into a \"pending\" list and only commits when...? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe pressing Done commits the typed subtask by inserting it into the DB immediately with a temporary negative ID and marks it \"new\" (parentId set). Then when the parent is saved, it updates the subtask. Hmm.\n\nLet's test a cleaner path: In Pack for trip editor, add subtask \"QA sub3\", then *delete the empty row* by tapping its Delete [after Done], then Save. Then create a new task. Check if QA sub3 detaches. This also tests the Delete button on a draft subtask.\n\nBut we already have lots of test artifacts. Let's plan cleanup at the end: delete QA sub, QA sub2, QA top, QA top2. QA sub3 if created.\n\nLet's proceed. Scroll to top? Pack is visible in current view partially? The latest observation has Charge phone at top (Pack above). Let's swipe down a bit to top? Actually we can tap Pack row if visible. The latest observation doesn't include Pack row. Swipe down 0.3."}
