{"type": "thinking", "text": "The list picker shows \"Local lists\" with \"Default list\" and an \"EditText Search\" field. It doesn't show checkmarks. Only one list exists (\"Default list\"). So all tasks are in Default list? Then why doesn't the Default list view show the original tasks?\n\nWait — maybe the \"Default list\" view we saw is actually filtered by something? It showed only the 4 QA tasks. Hmm. Could it be that the \"Default list\" drawer item is the **list filter** but the original tasks are in a different list named \"My Tasks\" (which isn't shown as a local list because it's the special default list). If the picker only offers \"Default list\" (a local list), then tasks in the \"My Tasks\" built-in list would have no list? Hmm.\n\nOh! I think I understand now: In Tasks.org, \"My Tasks\" is the default list (built-in). The local list \"Default list\" is a *newly created list* (maybe the test setup created it, or the app's \"Default list\" is a special name). The tasks in My Tasks might have listId = My Tasks (not offered in the picker because picker only shows local lists? no, it should show My Tasks too maybe under a \"Default list\" section).\n\nActually the picker shows only \"Local lists\" header and \"Default list\" item. If Buy milk were in My Tasks, the picker should show My Tasks at top (maybe under a \"Default\" section) with a checkmark. It doesn't. So Buy milk is in \"Default list\".\n\nThen the Default list view should show Buy milk. It didn't. That's a real filtering bug. Unless... the Default list view was showing only *tasks with no due date?* No, it showed \"No due date\" header only; maybe the due-date groups were above and the list was scrolled down! Wait, when I first navigated to Default list, the \"No due date\" header was at y=283 near the top. If the list had due-date tasks, they'd be above, and the list would be scrolled to bottom (No due date at top means scrolled). But we had just navigated to the list; it should start at top