{"type": "thinking", "text": "Filter persists after Save (header \"Default list\"). Good. So changed behavior works for list filters, at least this.\n\nBut the inconsistency remains: original tasks have chip \"Default list\" but aren't in the Default list view. Maybe that's because \"My Tasks\" is a special smart list that includes all tasks, and the \"Default list\" chip in the editor for original tasks is actually the **default list assigned when the task has no list** (the picker shows \"Default list\" because that's the only list). Hmm, but if Buy milk had no list, it wouldn't show in Default list. The chip may just display the default list's name as a placeholder, and saving with no changes doesn't assign it (since unchanged). That could explain it: Original tasks created without a list (i.e., in \"My Tasks\"/inbox). Their editor shows \"Default list\" as the default selection but saving leaves them listless if not touched? Hmm, but then the UI lies (shows Default list though not in it). That's a bug maybe, but the changed note about \"active list filter\" might relate.\n\nLet's test if the list chip for original tasks is *really* Default list: In the task editor for Buy milk, the chip showed \"Default list\" and the picker shows only Default list. There's no \"My Tasks\" option. So the app thinks Buy milk's list is Default list. But the Default list view doesn't show it. This could be a filter bug: the \"Default list\" view excludes tasks that are... maybe due to the **subtask badge change**? No.\n\nWait — maybe the \"Default list\" view is showing only tasks **not in the Default list**, i.e., the filter is inverted? It shows the 6 QA tasks which are in Default list... no.\n\nHmm, let's check if the original tasks appear in \"My Tasks\" only because My Tasks is not filtered by list. The preconditions just say \"the list contains: ...\" without naming it. The tasks might be in the My Tasks built-in list, and the QA tasks created in this session went to \"Default list\" because the new-task editor preselected \"Default list\" (while original tasks are in \"My Tasks\"). If original tasks were in My Tasks, their editor chip should show \"My Tasks\", but it shows \"Default list\". Unless the chip \"Default list\" is the *default list for new tasks* and the task's current list is \"My Tasks\" but the editor with \"active list filter reapplied\" **replaces** the task's list with the default list (because the active filter? no, active filter is My Tasks).\n\nHmm, maybe the changed behavior is: \"the active list filter is now reapplied whenever the editor is opened\" means when you open a task editor, the editor sets `task.list = activeListFilter`. If active filter is \"My Tasks\" (not a real list), it falls back to \"Default list\". That would explain the chip! And if you **save**, it might move the task to Default list. But our Save of Buy milk didn't move it (Default list count stayed 6). Hmm, unless it did move and the Default list view isn't showing due-date tasks due to a bug... Circular.\n\nLet's directly test whether saving Buy milk moves it:\n1. Note Default list count = 6.\n2. Go to My Tasks, open Buy milk, change something small (notes) and Save.\n3. Check Default list count and whether Buy milk appears.\n\nBut first, is Buy milk maybe already in Default list and the Default list view hides due-date tasks? We can test by changing QA top's due date: if the Default list view then hides QA top? Hmm.\n\nActually, let's check the Default list view with a fresh eye: It showed a \"No due date\" header and 6 tasks, all no due date. All QA tasks have no due date. Maybe the Default list view's due-date groups are **collapsed**? No, they'd still show headers. Unless the list filter only shows the \"No due date\" group because the tasks with due dates are in other lists. So indeed original tasks aren't in Default list.\n\nSo the question: are original tasks in \"My Tasks\" list or in \"Default list\"? Their editor chip says Default list. There's an inconsistency. Let's test by changing Buy milk's due date or notes and saving, then check Default list count. If it appears, chip is correct and Default list view was just not showing them (bug). If not, chip is misleading.\n\nActually, here's another thought: maybe when I created QA tasks, the default priority Medium and the current list \"Default list\" were applied. Original tasks were created in a list also called \"My Tasks\"? Hmm.\n\nLet's look at the drawer item counts: My Tasks 13 top-level, Default list 6. The 13 includes 7 original + 6 QA = 13. So My Tasks shows all 13 top-level tasks (it's a smart list \"all tasks\"). Default list shows 6 QA tasks. If original tasks were also in Default list, Default list count would be 13. So original tasks are not in Default list. Their editor chip \"Default list\" is therefore WRONG or they're in a different list with the same name. Maybe there are *two* lists both named \"Default list\": one built-in (containing original tasks) and one local (containing QA tasks). The drawer shows \"Local lists > Default list\" only; the built-in might be \"My Tasks\"? Hmm.\n\nIn Tasks.org, lists are per-account. The default list is called \"My Tasks\" but perhaps the local list name here is \"Default list\". Wait, the test environment might have created a local list named \"Default list\" as part of setup. Then original tasks might be in \"My Tasks\" (built-in) and the QA tasks went into the local \"Default list\" (because the new-task editor defaults to the last-used list, which was... hmm, but why would QA tasks go to local Default list?).\n\nActually, the new-task editor's list chip said \"Default list\" — that's the local list. So new tasks default to the local Default list. The original tasks' editor also shows \"Default list\" — which suggests the original tasks are in the local Default list too. Contradiction with counts.\n\nUnless the counts are stale! The drawer count might be cached. Hmm, but 6 was accurate.\n\nLet me open the list picker for an original task and see if there are *checkmarks* or multiple lists. The picker only showed a search field, \"Local lists\", \"Default list\", \"Create new list\". If the task were in My Tasks, the picker might show \"Default list\" as the default option without a checkmark and no indication of current list. So can't tell.\n\nLet's test with a small edit:\n- Go to My Tasks, open Buy milk, change notes from \"2 litres\" to \"2 litres \" (add a space) or change title to \"Buy milk\" (no change won't save). Save.\n- Then open drawer; check Default list count (6 or 7) and open Default list to see if Buy milk appears.\n\nThis modifies data but we can revert notes. Let's do it carefully. We should also eventually clean up QA tasks.\n\nWait, but maybe this \"list\" issue isn't one of the intended bugs and could be a rabbit hole. The changed note about \"active list filter\" specifically suggests testing filter reapplication in the editor. We tested:\n- Search filter persists after opening editor (Back and Save) — pass.\n- List filter (\"Default list\") persists after opening editor and Save — pass.\n- Maybe \"Today\" filter? Could test similarly. But likely pass.\n\nThe list membership inconsistency might be a real bug but hard to attribute. Let's not report it unless we can clearly articulate expected vs observed. The precondition says \"the list contains: ... Buy milk ...\". It doesn't specify which list; the main \"My Tasks\" list contains them. The \"Default list\" mismatch is odd but maybe by design (My Tasks is a smart list; local Default list is separate and the editor chip is the \"default list\" fallback). Hmm.\n\nHold on, maybe the editor chip for original tasks is not \"Default list\" as the task's list but the **\"Default list\" filter setting** because \"active list filter is now reapplied whenever the editor is opened\" — i.e., the change makes the editor's list chip show the active *list filter* (\"Default list\"?) Wait, active filter when opening from My Tasks should be My Tasks. Unless the active list filter is a saved filter defaulting to Default list, and the main screen is showing \"My Tasks\" smart list while the editor re-applies the \"Default list\" filter. Hmm.\n\nLet's test the changed behavior in a more targeted way: From the **Default list** screen, open a task whose list is *not* Defaul", "truncated": true}
