{"type": "thinking", "text": "Oops. Pressing BACK while search had an empty query exited the app entirely (went to launcher). That's interesting: The search screen was open (header search with empty query) and pressing BACK closed the app, rather than exiting search. Earlier, when a query was present and keyboard hidden, pressing BACK exited search to the main list. Now with empty query? Wait, after Save of Buy milk with empty query, the search screen remained. The search field was empty. Pressing BACK went to launcher — meaning the app exited, not returning to the main list. This could be the seeded bug! The changed note: \"Task list search: the system Back gesture during search is now handled by the main screen.\" Maybe the Back handling is broken when the search query is empty? Let's analyze.\n\nEarlier test: search \"milk\" (keyboard open) -> BACK (hides keyboard) -> BACK (exits search to main list). That worked. Now: search had empty query, keyboard hidden (after Save), I pressed BACK once and the app exited to launcher. So perhaps with an empty query, BACK does not exit search; it exits the app. Hmm, that's a bug: pressing Back in the search screen should return to the task list, not close the app.\n\nBut wait, in the first test, after search \"milk\" and pressing BACK twice, the second BACK exited search. That was with non-empty query. Maybe the Back handler only exits search if `searchView.hasFocus()` or keyboard shown? Here the search view may have lost focus after Save; the keyboard was hidden. The main screen handling may have popped the search fragment? Hmm.\n\nLet's reopen the app and test this scenario carefully:\n- open_app brings Tasks.org to foreground.\n- The search state may persist? \"open_app does not reset data\" and brings app to foreground; likely the search screen remains with empty query.\n- Press BACK and see.\n\nActually, from the launcher, the app's task might be in the background with the search screen still active (empty query). We can reopen and observe. Let's do open_app."}
