{"type": "thinking", "text": "Wait! With \"milk\" (non-empty query), pressing BACK after hiding keyboard ALSO exits the app now! Earlier it exited search to the list. What changed? Earlier, when we tested, pressing BACK with \"milk\" exited search and showed the main list. Let me re-check the earlier sequence:\n\nEarlier:\n- Search \"milk\"\n- first BACK: keyboard hidden, search still active.\n- second BACK: observation showed main list with 35 elements, title \"My Tasks\". So it exited search.\n\nNow:\n- Search \"milk\"\n- first BACK: keyboard hidden (13 elements).\n- second BACK: launcher. App exited.\n\nHmm, this is inconsistent. Maybe the earlier second BACK did exit search, but also finished the activity? No, the observation showed the main list (My Tasks) after the second BACK, so the activity was alive. So why now launcher?\n\nWait, maybe the launcher opened because the app was not the foreground? No, it was.\n\nCould the difference be that earlier the app had a navigation drawer state or something? Or maybe pressing BACK twice quickly? Let's consider: The first BACK (keyboard hide) and second BACK (exit search) — earlier we waited after each. Now the tool calls are immediate, maybe the second BACK was delivered before the search view had processed the first, causing the app to exit? Hmm, but each tool call returns after the UI settles, presumably.\n\nAlternatively, the behavior could be: pressing BACK with the search screen focused (even keyboard hidden) exits search; but if the search screen is empty, exits app. We got launcher for non-empty just now. Maybe because the query \"milk\" search screen was in a state where the back handler was not registered? Hmm.\n\nThis inconsistency might be due to Android's predictive back: pressing BACK while the IME is open hides the keyboard; pressing again should invoke the search view's back callback. But the timing might matter.\n\nLet me reproduce again with \"milk\" and a wait between backs. Open app, search \"milk\", BACK, wait 1s, BACK. See. This is worth pinning down because the bug could be exactly this: the Back gesture closes the app instead of exiting search, i.e., the new handling is broken.\n\nLet's open app, and repeat."}
